'use strict';
const {build,ref,NODE,EXPRESS}=require('./fullstack-wave3-helper');
const units=[
{comp:'m1.c1',title:'Trace synchronous work and promise continuations',before:'Complete JavaScript async/DOM and REST API design. Review call stacks, promises and HTTP request lifetimes.',intro:'A ticket API can await storage without blocking JavaScript, yet a long synchronous price calculation can delay every connected client in that process. The event loop coordinates readiness; it does not make arbitrary JavaScript run in parallel. Trace the stack before choosing a scaling remedy.',sections:[['Separate waiting from computation','A callback executes until it returns. Promise continuations execute after the current synchronous stack, before later event-loop callbacks in the usual simple example. An await suspends the async function, not the entire process. Independent requests may progress while an I/O operation is pending.','A CPU loop, synchronous filesystem call or huge JSON serialization still occupies the main thread. Adding async to its containing function changes the return type to a promise; it does not move the work to a worker. Bound input size and measure event-loop delay before attributing every latency problem to the database.'],['Choose evidence that matches the mechanism','A deterministic ordering demonstration proves scheduling relationships for the shown code, not throughput under production load. Timer delays are minimum thresholds and can run late when the loop is busy. Avoid teaching an exact wall-clock duration as a contract.','For CPU-heavy workloads, consider bounded worker-thread execution or a separate job service, including queue limits and cancellation. More processes can distribute requests across cores, but each process still has finite capacity. Never schedule unlimited workers from untrusted requests.']],problem:'Trace a request-shaped asynchronous calculation without opening a socket.',steps:['Log before creating a continuation.','Queue a resolved-promise handler.','Observe the synchronous log before the continuation.'],code:'const events=[];\nevents.push("start");\nPromise.resolve().then(()=>{events.push("promise");console.log(events.join(","));});\nevents.push("sync");',expect:'start,sync,promise',result:'The continuation follows synchronous work. This isolated example does not benchmark Node networking or worker parallelism.',mistakes:['An async keyword does not offload CPU work.','A timer threshold is not a deadline guarantee.'],takeaways:['Trace stack completion before queued continuations.','Separate CPU saturation from waiting on I/O.'],practice:[
'A handler contains a billion-iteration loop before await. What delays other callbacks?|The synchronous loop|The promise type alone|The URL spelling|A resolved HTTP header|JavaScript work holds the main thread until it yields.',
'What does await suspend in an ordinary async handler?|That async function|Every process on the host|The database engine|All worker threads|Other callbacks may execute while the function waits.',
'Which output order follows log A, Promise.then log B, log C?|A C B|A B C|B A C|C B A|The synchronous stack emits A and C before the queued continuation.',
'Why can a 10 ms timer fire much later under load?|Callbacks wait for the event loop|Timers reserve a dedicated CPU|Milliseconds become seconds|Promises disable timers|A threshold does not preempt currently running JavaScript.'],formal:[
'An invoice API becomes unresponsive during synchronous PDF preparation. Which remedy targets CPU contention?|Bounded worker execution|Adding async to the same loop|Increasing the query timeout only|Removing request ids|Moving substantial computation off the request thread can restore event-loop capacity.',
'A server awaits a network read. What can its JavaScript thread do meanwhile?|Run other ready callbacks|Execute the suspended function continuously|Guarantee the response time|Perform no work until the read ends|Suspending one async function permits the event loop to handle other ready work.',
'What is a promise continuation in this runtime model?|Work queued after stack completion|A new operating-system process|An automatic database transaction|A synchronous exception handler only|Then handlers are scheduled continuations rather than immediate calls on the current stack.',
'A dashboard shows high event-loop delay and high CPU during JSON encoding. Where should investigation begin?|Serialization cost|Only remote database indexes|The number of CSS files|Browser cache expiration|Synchronous serialization can delay unrelated server callbacks.',
'A developer wraps a blocking hash loop in Promise.resolve().then. What remains true?|The loop still uses the main thread|The loop always runs in parallel|A separate process is created|CPU cost disappears|A promise changes scheduling without supplying a worker.',
'A callback schedules a timeout and then performs heavy synchronous work. Which statement is defensible?|The timeout may run after the work ends|The timeout interrupts the callback|The timeout always runs at its exact threshold|Node kills the callback automatically|A timer callback needs the event loop to regain control.',
'A service spawns one worker per upload without a cap. What must be added?|Bounded admission|Unlimited retry attempts|A longer response string|A second uncontrolled worker pool|Workers consume finite resources and need admission control.',
'A report API has low database time, high main-thread CPU and p99 loop delay during giant JSON responses. Adding replicas lowers average load but one large response still stalls its process. Which redesign directly addresses the measured cause?|Bound payload and CPU work|Raise the database timeout|Queue every request forever|Add async to serialization|Bounded payloads and worker or job boundaries address synchronous main-thread pressure; replicas alone do not remove a large per-request stall.']},
{comp:'m1.c2',title:'Load modules and validate configuration at startup',before:'Use require, object destructuring and environment string values. Configuration is input and needs a contract.',intro:'A payroll service should reject an invalid listening port before it accepts work. Module loading and configuration belong to a deliberate startup phase. An exported singleton can be shared by imports in one process, while environment variables remain untyped strings until the application validates them.',sections:[['Make package and module boundaries explicit','CommonJS uses require and module.exports; ECMAScript modules use import and export with Node rules determined by extensions and package type. A package lock records a resolved dependency graph. npm ci expects consistency with package.json and does not repair a stale lock as part of a reproducible build.','Require resolves a module and commonly caches it by resolved filename within a process. A mutable exported object can therefore become shared process state. It is not a durable cross-process database. Keep reusable rules independent of reading environment values at import time when test configuration needs isolation.'],['Fail clearly before serving traffic','Read required configuration once, validate the complete lexical form and range, and pass a typed settings object into application construction. parseInt accepts prefixes such as 3000oops; Number plus a deliberate decimal-string rule avoids accepting accidental trailing characters.','Secrets should come from an appropriate secret source and must not be embedded in a browser bundle or diagnostic dump. Log the setting name and safe failure category, not the secret value. Use separate configuration for local fixtures and any deployed environment; this lesson performs no deployment.']],problem:'Accept a valid decimal port and reject a mixed string before application construction.',steps:['Validate the entire string.','Convert and enforce the network-port range.','Exercise good and bad synthetic settings.'],code:'function port(raw){if(!/^\\d+$/.test(raw))throw Error("invalid port");const n=Number(raw);if(!Number.isInteger(n)||n<1||n>65535)throw Error("invalid port");return n;}\nconsole.log(port("3000"));\ntry{port("3000oops");}catch(e){console.log(e.message);}',expect:'3000\ninvalid port',result:'The pure parser rejects trailing text. No environment secrets are read and no listener is created.',mistakes:['parseInt alone accepts a numeric prefix.','An exported mutable singleton is not shared durable storage.'],takeaways:['Validate configuration before serving requests.','Keep module-system rules and dependency resolution explicit.'],practice:[
'Environment variable PORT is read from process.env. What type is a provided value initially?|String|Guaranteed integer|TCP socket|Promise|Environment values require explicit conversion and validation.',
'Why reject 3000oops when parsing a port?|Malformed full value|TCP supports no decimal values|It is a valid secret|Node cannot compare integers|A prefix parse would hide malformed configuration.',
'Which command uses an existing consistent npm lock for a clean installation?|npm ci|npm init|npm publish|npm version major|npm ci installs the locked graph and requires manifest consistency.',
'Two require calls resolve the same CommonJS file in one process. What commonly happens?|Cached exports are reused|Two database servers start automatically|Exports become immutable|The file becomes public|CommonJS caching can share an exported object in that process.',
'A test mutates an imported settings object and another test fails. What boundary was shared?|Process-local module state|A mandatory remote cache|Browser storage|A Git commit|Cached mutable exports can leak changes between tests.',
'Which setting should never be compiled into a public frontend bundle?|A database password|A public support URL|A product title|A nonsecret locale|Browser-delivered code is available to its recipients.',
'What should a startup error report for a missing secret?|The setting name only|The raw credential|Every environment variable|The database backup|A useful diagnostic can identify the missing setting without exposing credentials.',
'An ESM file uses module.exports without a compatibility boundary. What needs review?|The selected module system|The HTTP status phrase|The number of processor cores|The schema table order|CommonJS and ESM syntax follow different loading rules.'],formal:[
'What does package-lock.json primarily record?|Resolved dependency graph|Production secret values|Every HTTP request|The host firewall rules|The lockfile records the package resolution used for repeatable installation.',
'Why construct a typed settings object once at startup?|Central validation|Make all strings secret|Replace authorization|Guarantee zero runtime errors|Startup validation catches invalid settings before requests rely on them.',
'A clean CI run has a manifest that disagrees with its lockfile. What should npm ci do?|Fail on the mismatch|Publish a new package|Ignore the manifest|Read production credentials|A clean locked installation requires consistency instead of silently rewriting resolution.',
'A worker process imports the same module as the API process. Is their exported Map a shared durable store?|No, each process has its own memory|Yes, require uses global host memory|Yes, package locks replicate it|Yes, module.exports persists to disk|Module caching is local to the relevant process and does not supply distributed persistence.',
'A service accepts PORT=0 despite an explicit 1–65535 application policy. What is defective?|Range validation|The HTTP method|The CommonJS cache|The SQL placeholder|A syntactically numeric string can still violate the application range.',
'A module reads process.env at import and freezes it before a test sets fixtures. What design improves testability?|Inject validated configuration|Add more hidden import reads|Publish the environment file|Depend on test ordering|Explicit configuration inputs remove hidden timing dependencies.',
'A build copies a server credential into frontend configuration. What must change?|Keep it server-side|Minify it more aggressively|Rename the variable only|Put it in a source map|Browser assets cannot keep a server credential confidential.',
'A package changes to ESM while startup imports configure a singleton pool and tests require CommonJS helpers. What migration review prevents both loading and lifecycle regressions?|Audit syntax and side effects|Only rename package files|Only add default exports|Start a pool on every import|The module boundary and import side effects both change operational behavior; a syntax-only conversion can retain uncontrolled resource ownership.']},
{comp:'m1.c1',title:'Apply backpressure and bounded asynchronous work',before:'Review the event-loop trace and distinguish bounded concurrency from sequential and unlimited execution.',intro:'An export endpoint that loads every student record at once can exhaust memory even though its database calls are asynchronous. Backpressure asks the producer to slow down when the consumer cannot keep up. Concurrency limits make resource budgets explicit rather than equating async with unlimited capacity.',sections:[['Bound the amount of work in flight','Promise.all starts no work by itself, but mapping an async operation over every record usually initiates them all before awaiting their combined result. For a large collection this can overload connections and memory. Choose a batch size or worker limit based on dependency capacity and the response deadline.','Node writable streams return false from write when buffered data reaches the high-water threshold; the producer should wait for drain before continuing. That threshold is a buffering signal, not a hard universal memory cap. Errors and early disconnects still require cleanup.'],['Design overload behavior intentionally','A queue should have a maximum size and a policy for rejection or deferral. Otherwise latency grows until clients retry, multiplying load. A batch export may belong in a job system with a durable status resource instead of a long open request.','The example models a two-item batch policy with deterministic results. It does not implement Node streams or prove network throughput. For real streams, use documented pipeline error handling and cancellation, then test slow consumers and dropped connections in an isolated integration fixture.']],problem:'Process five synthetic records in bounded batches and preserve result order.',steps:['Partition inputs into groups of at most two.','Await each batch before admitting the next.','Inspect the final ordered values.'],code:'(async()=>{const values=[1,2,3,4,5],out=[];for(let i=0;i<values.length;i+=2){out.push(...await Promise.all(values.slice(i,i+2).map(async x=>x*2)));}console.log(out.join(","));})();',expect:'2,4,6,8,10',result:'No batch exceeds two operations in this model. It does not measure a live connection pool or stream.',mistakes:['Mapping every item before batching defeats admission control.','A stream buffer signal does not replace error handling.'],takeaways:['Bound queued and active work separately.','Slow the producer when the consumer cannot keep up.'],practice:[
'A request maps 100000 async queries into Promise.all. What risk appears?|Excessive in-flight work|Guaranteed sequential execution|Automatic pool expansion to infinity|No memory consumption|Creating all operations can overload finite dependencies.',
'A writable stream write returns false. What should the producer normally wait for?|drain|A CSS event|A new JWT|A SQL migration|The stream signals that buffering should pause until capacity returns.',
'Why bound a request queue as well as worker count?|Waiting work costs memory|Only active work uses resources|Queues cannot grow|It makes retries idempotent|A finite worker count alone does not cap waiting demand.',
'When should an export become an asynchronous job?|For long-running exports|Whenever output is a string|Only when JavaScript is synchronous|Never if promises exist|A durable job can expose progress without holding one long response open.'],formal:[
'What does backpressure coordinate?|Producer/consumer rate|User roles with password length|Git tags with branches|Only HTTP status spelling|Backpressure prevents a producer from indefinitely outrunning its consumer.',
'Which memory quantity can an unbounded queue increase?|Waiting payloads|Only executable file size|Only CSS cache size|No application memory|Queued request or job data occupies memory even before execution.',
'A bulk API opens one query for every uploaded row before awaiting. What change limits pressure?|Admit a bounded number at a time|Await the already-created full array|Increase uploaded row count|Remove connection release|Admission must happen before creating unlimited dependency work.',
'A client stops downloading a report. What should the server consider?|Cancel production and release resources|Continue unlimited generation forever|Mark every job successful|Double the stream buffer repeatedly|Disconnected consumers no longer justify unbounded ongoing work.',
'A stream producer ignores false from write. What is the likely capacity problem?|Growing buffered data|Automatic lossless infinite storage|Guaranteed lower latency|A syntax error in every write|Ignoring the signal can accumulate memory while the consumer remains slow.',
'A team chooses concurrency equal to ten times the database pool size. What needs validation?|Capacity and queue delay|Only output capitalization|The number of route files|The project license|The useful limit must reflect actual resource capacity and latency budgets.',
'A batch export must survive API restarts and show progress. Which boundary is appropriate?|Durable job status|An in-memory promise alone|A browser alert only|A longer synchronous loop|Durability and independent status require a persistent job boundary.',
'An export API limits active workers but accepts an unbounded queue; retry storms now exhaust memory. Which combined policy protects useful work under sustained overload?|Bound queue and admission|Only increase worker count|Only lengthen client waits|Create one worker per retry|Active concurrency and waiting work both need finite budgets, with a declared overload outcome and client retry discipline.']},
{comp:'m2.c1',title:'Compose Express middleware in the intended order',before:'Know HTTP methods and statuses. Express 4 middleware receives req, res and next; this lesson uses a bounded chain model.',intro:'An enrollment API must attach identity before checking access and must parse a JSON body before validating its fields. Express processes matching middleware in registration order. A misplaced responder can prevent later checks from running, so ordering is part of the security and behavior contract.',sections:[['Trace the chain one transfer at a time','app.use can mount middleware for a path prefix, while a route also selects a method and path. Calling next transfers control; sending a response normally ends that request path. Middleware should either respond or delegate once. Falling through without either action can leave a request pending.','Register a body parser before handlers that consume req.body. Identity middleware must establish trusted context before authorization uses it. Put a terminal not-found handler after the intended routes, and error middleware after handlers that can delegate errors.'],['Distinguish a model from the framework','The local array below demonstrates order and delegation, not Express path matching. The pinned Express 4 application/router sources define actual behavior. Integration tests should exercise real middleware ordering, request parsing, content types and status codes with an isolated server fixture.','A middleware that writes part of a response and later calls next can make a second responder fail with headers already sent. Keep ownership of the response explicit. Instrument safe request ids at the start of the chain so both successful and failing paths can be correlated.']],problem:'Model trusted identity establishment before authorization.',steps:['Create an empty synthetic request.','Run identity before the access check.','Verify the terminal responder sees the allowed flag.'],code:'const req={},trace=[];\nconst chain=[(r,n)=>{r.user={id:"u1"};trace.push("identity");n();},(r,n)=>{r.allowed=!!r.user;trace.push("access");n();},r=>trace.push(String(r.allowed))];\nlet i=0;function next(){chain[i++](req,next);}next();console.log(trace.join(","));',expect:'identity,access,true',result:'The explicit chain order establishes context first. No Express routing, socket or authentication provider executes here.',mistakes:['A responder before authorization can bypass later checks.','A middleware that neither responds nor delegates leaves the path unfinished.'],takeaways:['Treat registration order as part of behavior.','Delegate or respond once with clear response ownership.'],practice:[
'Which Express argument normally delegates to later middleware?|next|res.json alone|req.params alone|app.listen alone|Calling next transfers control through the registered chain.',
'Where should JSON parsing occur relative to body validation?|Before validation|Only after response completion|Only in the browser|After a terminal 404|The validator requires parsed input before it can examine fields.',
'What can happen if middleware neither responds nor calls next?|The request remains pending|The framework always returns 201|Authorization automatically succeeds|A database transaction is committed|The chain has no completion or transfer path.',
'Why attach request ids near the beginning of the chain?|Correlate later outcomes|To authorize all users|To encrypt the body|To replace input validation|Early safe correlation context supports both success and error diagnostics.'],formal:[
'What primarily determines middleware sequence in Express 4?|Registration order|Alphabetical function names|Response body length|Package-lock ordering|Matching middleware is traversed in registration order.',
'What is the role of a terminal not-found handler?|Handle unmatched routes|Parse every JSON body first|Create a user identity|Guarantee a database connection|The final not-found path handles unmatched requests after route opportunities.',
'A route sends private data before access middleware is reached. What correction is required?|Authorize before sending|Add a later logging callback only|Rename the response field|Increase the timeout|A completed response cannot be protected by a check that occurs afterward.',
'A body validator sees undefined because express.json is mounted later. What fixes the chain?|Mount the parser earlier|Coerce undefined to an approved body|Disable validation|Send two responses|The parser must execute before the validator uses req.body.',
'A middleware sends JSON then delegates to another JSON responder. What risk follows?|A second response attempt|Automatic response merging|Implicit transaction rollback|Automatic authentication|A downstream responder may attempt to write headers or body after completion.',
'An access check runs before identity middleware. What contract is missing?|Trusted identity context|A public stylesheet|A shorter route name|A package version comment|Authorization must operate on an established trusted principal.',
'A chain model passes but Express requests fail on path mounting. What evidence is missing?|Framework integration|Another identical array trace|A screenshot of source text|Only more arithmetic tests|A simplified chain does not implement Express path matching and mounting.',
'A private route is mounted behind identity middleware, but an earlier cache responder serves by URL alone and never calls next. Which security review targets the actual bypass?|Check cache before access|Only rename the middleware|Only add an error logger|Only change JSON formatting|A responder can terminate the chain before authorization; cache keys and placement must preserve identity scope before private data is served.']},
{comp:'m2.c2',title:'Parse, validate and project request data',before:'Review JSON types, path parameters and REST validation responses. Parsing and business validation are different stages.',intro:'A warehouse endpoint receives an item id in the path and a quantity in JSON. Successful parsing only means the bytes formed valid JSON; it does not establish that the quantity is an integer, the item belongs to the caller or extra fields are safe to persist.',sections:[['Validate types and bounds at the boundary','Express route parameters are strings. Query values and parsed body properties remain untrusted. Validate object shape, required fields, numeric ranges and length bounds before calling business logic. Decide whether unknown properties are rejected or stripped; never copy arbitrary input into a database update.','Treat content-type and parser limits as explicit API policy. A huge valid JSON body can consume memory before business validation runs. express.json has a configurable limit; request size and rate policies should be tested alongside malformed JSON behavior.'],['Normalize deliberately without losing meaning','Number.isInteger rejects strings even when they look numeric, which is appropriate for a JSON schema requiring a number. If coercion is supported, define it deliberately and reject ambiguous forms rather than relying on broad truthiness. Zero may be a valid value under one contract and invalid under another.','Projection builds an allowlisted command object so internal columns such as ownerId or approved cannot be set through mass assignment. Authorization still checks the requested resource using trusted identity; validation alone does not prove the caller may edit it.']],problem:'Accept a bounded numeric quantity and project only permitted fields.',steps:['Require a plain object-shaped input.','Check integer quantity within the declared range.','Construct a new command without privileged extra fields.'],code:'function command(body){if(!body||Array.isArray(body)||typeof body!=="object"||!Number.isInteger(body.quantity)||body.quantity<1||body.quantity>20)throw Error("invalid");return {quantity:body.quantity};}\nconsole.log(JSON.stringify(command({quantity:2,approved:true})));\ntry{command({quantity:"2"});}catch(e){console.log(e.message);}',expect:'{"quantity":2}\ninvalid',result:'The model strips approved and rejects a numeric string under a numeric JSON contract. It does not authenticate or authorize a real resource.',mistakes:['Valid JSON is not valid business input.','Spreading req.body into a write permits mass assignment.'],takeaways:['Validate type, shape and range separately.','Pass an allowlisted command across the business boundary.'],practice:[
'An Express path parameter represents order id 12. What type is it normally initially?|String|Guaranteed integer|Database row|Boolean|Path parameters require deliberate validation and conversion.',
'An Express endpoint parses a body containing an order quantity of minus five and an arbitrary owner ID without throwing. What has the parser alone established?|Syntactically valid JSON|Authorized ownership|Valid stock quantity|Safe persistence of all fields|Parsing does not establish the application schema or permission.',
'Why construct an allowlisted update object?|Limit writable fields|Encrypt every value|Eliminate all races|Replace authentication|Projection limits which input properties reach the write boundary.',
'Which check enforces a JSON numeric integer contract?|Number.isInteger(value)|Boolean(value)|parseInt(value) alone|value.length alone|An integer check rejects numeric strings and fractional numbers.',
'A client sets approved:true beside a valid quantity. What should a quantity-only endpoint do?|Strip or reject it|Persist the whole body|Trust it because JSON parsed|Move it into a token|The endpoint must enforce its allowlisted writable fields.',
'Why configure a JSON body size limit?|Limit parser memory use|Guarantee user ownership|Make every request valid|Replace database constraints|Even syntactically valid large bodies can consume excessive memory.',
'An input object has quantity 0 and the contract requires 1–20. What fails?|The range rule|JSON syntax|The module loader|The route method automatically|A valid JSON number may still violate a business bound.',
'Does validating itemId prove a caller owns that item?|No; check ownership too|Yes, if the id is numeric|Yes, if JSON is valid|Yes, if the id is short|Input validity and permission are different requirements.'],formal:[
'Which stage identifies malformed JSON syntax?|Body parsing|Database ownership authorization|Response caching|Package resolution|The parser must first convert valid JSON bytes into values.',
'What does a schema validator add beyond parsing?|Shape/value constraints|Proof of user identity|Database availability|Cross-process locking|Application schema rules constrain the values produced by parsing.',
'A stock route accepts quantity 1.5 although only whole units are allowed. Which rule is missing?|Integer validation|A longer path prefix|A second content type|A worker-thread pool|Whole-unit quantities require integer rather than merely numeric validation.',
'A client submits role=admin to a profile edit route that spreads the request body. What is the defect?|Mass assignment|An ordinary read cache hit|A timer scheduling problem|An unavoidable JSON feature|Unrestricted input copying can write fields the caller must not control.',
'A valid request body is a top-level array where an object is required. What should validation do?|Reject the wrong shape|Treat every array as an approved record|Persist its indexes as columns|Infer authorization from length|JSON validity does not imply the expected application shape.',
'A quantity-only command strips an extra property. Does that complete resource security?|No, ownership must still be enforced|Yes, every stripped object is authorized|Yes, the parser is an authenticator|Only if the property name is short|Allowlisting write fields does not establish permission on the target resource.',
'A gateway permits a 50 MB JSON body but the route needs only two small fields. What policy deserves attention?|Request size bounds|Only response indentation|The number of route files|A larger module cache|Early size limits reduce unnecessary parser resource pressure.',
'An endpoint validates quantity but spreads remaining body fields into a tenant-scoped update. A caller includes ownerId and approved, while the row id is otherwise authorized. Which boundary is still unsafe?|Writable-field projection|Only integer validation|Only response compression|Only pool connection reuse|Resource access does not grant permission to mutate privileged fields; construct the allowed command independently of the whole body.']},
{comp:'m2.c1',title:'Separate route construction from server startup',before:'Review middleware ordering. app construction and listening are separate responsibilities in an Express service.',intro:'A booking application needs fast route tests and one shared process-level listener. If importing a module starts the server, each test import can acquire ports or initialize operational resources. Build the app through an explicit factory, then start the listener in a thin entry point.',sections:[['Inject collaborators into application construction','A createApp function can accept a repository, clock and safe logger, install middleware and return the Express application. A separate entry point validates settings, builds concrete adapters and calls listen. Importing business rules should not unexpectedly bind sockets or execute migrations.','A Router groups related routes and can be mounted under a prefix. Its URL parameters and mount behavior belong to the framework contract and need real request tests. A reusable router should accept the dependencies it uses rather than silently constructing an uncontrolled global database pool.'],['Test the correct boundary','A direct handler test verifies local branching and projection. A real HTTP integration test additionally checks parser behavior, route matching and middleware order. Keep both evidence types honest. Dependency injection enables synthetic repositories without claiming that the target database was exercised.','Shutdown must stop accepting work and eventually release server and dependency resources. This lesson only demonstrates factory injection through a pure adapter model. Starting and stopping an actual listener requires an isolated fixture with bounded lifecycle and is not performed by the snippet.']],problem:'Construct two independent route-shaped adapters with distinct repositories.',steps:['Define a factory that accepts its dependency.','Build adapters with different synthetic data.','Show imports need not start a server.'],code:'function createHandler(repo){return id=>({status:200,body:repo.find(id)});}\nconst a=createHandler({find:id=>({id,site:"A"})});\nconst b=createHandler({find:id=>({id,site:"B"})});\nconsole.log(a("x").body.site,b("x").body.site);',expect:'A B',result:'Separate injected dependencies produce independent observations. This is a factory model rather than an Express HTTP integration test.',mistakes:['Starting listeners during imports makes test lifecycle implicit.','A fake repository does not verify a real database adapter.'],takeaways:['Construct the app separately from listening.','Inject adapters and verify real seams with isolated integration tests.'],practice:[
'Why keep app.listen outside a reusable app factory?|Explicit lifecycle control|Guarantee no errors ever occur|Eliminate route matching|Make all handlers synchronous|Importing an application should not unexpectedly acquire a listening socket.',
'What does dependency injection make explicit in a route factory?|Its required collaborator|Every database index|The client screen size|The production credential|An injected repository exposes the route boundary for controlled tests.',
'What additional seam does a real HTTP route test cover?|Framework parsing and matching|Production traffic automatically|All database dialects|Human approval|A direct handler call does not exercise the actual HTTP middleware path.',
'An imported router creates its own pool in every test. What improvement helps lifecycle control?|Inject the managed pool|Create more hidden pools|Ignore leaked connections|Require a public database|Explicit ownership allows setup and teardown to be bounded.'],formal:[
'What should an Express application factory usually return?|A configured application|A published cloud service|A human review certificate|A production database backup|Application construction can be separated from listener startup and deployment.',
'Why use an Express Router?|Group and mount related routes|Provide automatic SQL transactions|Encrypt every response|Avoid all middleware|Routers organize route and middleware boundaries under explicit mounts.',
'A unit test passes with a fake repository but the JSON parser rejects the real request. What test boundary is missing?|An isolated HTTP integration test|Another identical fake call|Only a business-rule snapshot|A source line count|Parser and middleware behavior require the actual framework path.',
'A module starts listening as soon as tests require it. What failure can result?|Port and lifecycle conflicts|Automatic route validation|Guaranteed parallel isolation|A durable job queue|Implicit listeners complicate concurrent tests and resource cleanup.',
'A service is shutting down while requests are active. Which concern belongs in lifecycle design?|Stop admission and bound cleanup|Accept new work forever|Forget dependency pools|Run destructive migrations automatically|Graceful shutdown requires bounded treatment of active work and owned resources.',
'Two app fixtures unexpectedly share a global mutable repository. What change improves isolation?|Inject separate state|Rename the global variable|Share more mutable fixtures|Depend on test order|Separate injected state avoids cross-fixture interference.',
'A route-shaped function returns a status object without Express. What may it honestly demonstrate?|Local adapter behavior|Live socket routing|TLS configuration|Actual database pooling|The evidence is limited to the components that actually executed.',
'A route-factory unit test passes with a fake repository, but the deployed mount prefix, JSON limit and authentication order differ. What minimal additional boundary supplies the missing evidence?|Real app request fixture|Another direct handler call|Only repository fake tests|Only source-level snapshots|A constructed real application request test exercises mounting, parsing and middleware sequencing together; isolated function tests cannot prove these seams.']},
{comp:'m3.c1',title:'Forward rejected promises in Express 4',before:'Know async functions return promises and await can throw a rejection into the surrounding async function.',intro:'A customer lookup can reject after the route has returned its promise. In Express 4, that rejected promise must be explicitly forwarded to next. A synchronous try/catch around an unawaited asynchronous call does not handle the later rejection.',sections:[['Connect the async boundary to framework errors','Express 4 catches synchronous throws in invoked handlers, while rejected async handlers need an explicit catch(next) pattern or a wrapper that performs the equivalent transfer. Express 5 adds automatic forwarding for returned rejected promises; version-specific behavior matters when reviewing examples.','An async function can use try/await/catch and call next(error). A reusable wrapper should return the promise and forward the error once. Avoid both calling next(error) and rethrowing into another error-forwarding layer, which can dispatch the same failure twice.'],['Preserve the contract after a failure','Once an operation fails, stop the success response path. A catch that only logs and continues can send a misleading 200. A wrapper does not cancel external work or make writes transactional; it only connects rejection to the framework error channel.','The fixture executes a promise wrapper with synthetic response and next collaborators. It proves one rejected promise reaches the supplied error callback. It does not verify Express dispatch, a live database or network behavior. Use a real isolated request test for those seams.']],problem:'Forward one rejected asynchronous handler into a synthetic error callback.',steps:['Wrap an async function with a returned promise.','Convert rejection into one next call.','Inspect the captured failure category.'],code:'(async()=>{const seen=[];const wrap=fn=>(req,res,next)=>Promise.resolve().then(()=>fn(req,res,next)).catch(next);await wrap(async()=>{throw Error("lookup failed");})({}, {},e=>seen.push(e.message));console.log(seen.length,seen[0]);})();',expect:'1 lookup failed',result:'One rejection is captured by the callback. The snippet models the Express 4 forwarding pattern without loading Express.',mistakes:['Express 5 promise forwarding must not be assumed in Express 4.','Logging a rejection is not a successful response.'],takeaways:['Return and forward the rejected promise explicitly in Express 4.','Transfer each failure once and stop the success path.'],practice:[
'An Express 4 async handler rejects without catch(next). What is missing?|Explicit error forwarding|A longer route name|A CSS loader|Automatic database commit|Express 4 does not provide the Express 5 returned-promise forwarding contract.',
'Why does try{fetchData()} fail to catch a later rejection without await?|Rejection happens later|Promises cannot reject|Try blocks disable async work|Express parses the promise as JSON|The rejection must be awaited or chained to a rejection handler.',
'What should an async wrapper usually return?|The forwarded promise chain|A random boolean only|A secret environment dump|A listening port|Returning the chain makes completion and failure observable to its caller.',
'A catch logs a failed lookup then sends 200 success. What is defective?|False success reporting|The log has too few colors|The URL is too short|The module system is always wrong|Failure handling must preserve the endpoint business contract.'],formal:[
'Which Express version behavior does this course explicitly target?|Express 4 explicit promise forwarding|Every framework version identically|Only browser fetch semantics|Automatic SQL rollback on rejection|The pinned framework version requires explicit handling of returned promise rejections.',
'A handler awaits a rejected repository call inside try/catch. What connects it to Express 4 error middleware?|next(error)|res.status(200) alone|Calling the handler again|Ignoring the caught value|next(error) transfers the failure into Express error handling.',
'A wrapper both calls next(error) and rethrows to another catch(next). What risk occurs?|Duplicate error delegation|Automatic successful retry|Database isolation|Guaranteed response merging|The same failure can be forwarded through two paths.',
'An async route wrapper fixes rejection forwarding. Which property remains unproved?|Database transaction correctness|The catch callback was invoked|A promise was returned|The error message was captured|A wrapper controls error transfer rather than data atomicity.',
'A route continues its normal response after next(error). What should be corrected?|Stop the success path|Add another success response|Delete the error middleware|Retry without a limit|Error delegation should not be followed by conflicting success work.',
'A test only checks a resolved async route. What missing case exposes forwarding defects?|A controlled rejection|A larger successful response|Another route filename|A second identical resolved call|The error channel must be exercised with an actual rejected operation.',
'A shared async wrapper calls next on rejection and then rethrows; a second wrapper catches the returned promise. Some requests now hit error middleware twice. Which correction preserves the intended failure contract?|One forwarding owner|Two success responses|An unhandled rejection|A longer response timeout|Exactly one layer should own conversion of the rejected promise into next(error), while returning an observable chain and stopping success work.']},
{comp:'m3.c2',title:'Map failures through centralized error middleware',before:'Review next(error). Express error middleware uses four parameters and follows the routes that delegate failures.',intro:'A subscription API should return a safe validation response for invalid input and a generic internal error for an unexpected adapter failure. Centralized error middleware maps known categories consistently while preserving operational diagnostics without disclosing raw stacks or credentials to clients.',sections:[['Keep the framework signature and ordering','Express identifies error middleware by its error, req, res, next signature. Install it after routes that can fail. A missing fourth parameter can turn a function into ordinary middleware rather than the intended error handler. Preserve the explicit signature even if one parameter is unused.','Use a controlled public error taxonomy. Distinguish known input conflicts from unexpected internal failures; do not treat every error that happens to carry status as trusted. The public message should be stable and safe, while a controlled logger records the cause and request identifier.'],['Respect response state','If headers have already been sent, a centralized responder cannot start a second JSON response. Express documentation recommends delegating to the default error handler with next(error) in that state. For streaming routes, design errors before and after response commitment separately.','This pure mapping example has no Express response object. It demonstrates safe projection of known errors and generic handling for unexpected failures. An actual HTTP test must verify headersSent behavior, content type and middleware dispatch under the pinned framework.']],problem:'Map known input errors without exposing an unexpected internal message.',steps:['Recognize a controlled public error kind.','Choose status and safe text.','Verify internal failure details are not projected.'],code:'function publicError(e){return e.kind==="input"?{status:400,error:"Invalid request"}:{status:500,error:"Internal error"};}\nconsole.log(JSON.stringify(publicError({kind:"input",message:"bad quantity"})));\nconsole.log(JSON.stringify(publicError(new Error("secret database detail"))));',expect:'{"status":400,"error":"Invalid request"}\n{"status":500,"error":"Internal error"}',result:'Only approved public categories appear. No framework dispatcher or logging service was exercised.',mistakes:['A three-parameter function is not the intended Express error signature.','Internal exception messages can expose sensitive implementation details.'],takeaways:['Centralize a controlled status and message taxonomy.','Handle already-committed responses through the framework error path.'],practice:[
'Which Express error middleware signature is required?|(err,req,res,next)|(req,res,next)|(req,res)|(body,status)|The four-argument signature identifies the error middleware role.',
'Where should centralized error middleware normally be registered?|After failing routes|Only before the body parser|Inside a public JSON asset|In the browser bundle|Downstream error middleware receives failures from earlier matching handlers.',
'What should a client receive for an unexpected internal failure?|A safe generic error|A raw database password|The full environment file|A private stack trace|Public failure information should not disclose operational internals.',
'What does res.headersSent indicate?|Headers are committed|All database writes succeeded|The user is authorized|The response body was validated|A new JSON response cannot safely start after headers have been sent.',
'A stream fails after headers are sent. What should standard error middleware do?|Delegate via next(error)|Start a second JSON response|Change the already-sent status to 400|Claim success automatically|The default error path must handle the already-committed response state.',
'Why record safe request ids alongside internal failures?|Correlate layer events|Replace the error status|Authorize the caller|Expose the whole body|Correlation connects operational evidence without requiring secret payload disclosure.',
'An error object from an untrusted source carries status=200. Should it directly control the public response?|No, map controlled error categories|Yes, every status field is authoritative|Yes, if the message is long|Only on GET requests|The application must control which failure categories and statuses it exposes.',
'A centralized mapper returns 409 for a known edit conflict. What should the client do?|Treat it as a conflict outcome|Assume the edit committed successfully|Retry forever without a budget|Read the server stack trace|A conflict status represents a known failure to satisfy the write precondition.'],formal:[
'What is centralized error mapping responsible for?|Consistent safe failure responses|Automatically fixing all failed writes|Granting object access|Running every request again|A mapper translates recognized failures into stable public outcomes.',
'A route delegates an error but the error function has only three parameters. What should review inspect?|Four-argument signature|Only the function name|The number of imports|Only the HTTP hostname|Express distinguishes error middleware using its four-argument form.',
'A service returns database connection details in a 500 body. What correction is needed?|Safe public projection|Return the secret with 200 instead|Encode the secret in base64|Move it into a response header|Encoding or repositioning sensitive data does not make public disclosure safe.',
'A logger and error mapper disagree on request identity. What should be propagated consistently?|A bounded safe correlation id|A database password|Every raw cookie|A public private-key value|A shared safe id links operational events for one request.',
'A handler has already written part of a download before failing. Why can ordinary JSON error output be invalid?|Response commitment|JSON never supports errors|All streams are synchronous|HTTP has no status codes|Headers and body ownership constrain what can be sent after commitment.',
'A mapper classifies every thrown object as a client validation problem. What risk results?|Misclassified failures|Every fault is repaired|Metrics become automatically accurate|Clients gain less latency always|A controlled taxonomy must distinguish internal failures from known input errors.',
'A streaming route fails after writing headers; centralized error middleware tries JSON and another handler attempts a second response. Which design correctly respects both phases?|Branch on headersSent|Always send fresh JSON|Always change status to 500|Ignore errors after writes|Before commitment the mapper may emit a safe response; after commitment it must delegate through the framework error path rather than start another body.']},
{comp:'m3.c1',title:'Handle partial failure without duplicating a write',before:'Review rejected promises and safe error mapping. A failed response does not necessarily mean a write failed.',intro:'An enrollment write may commit before its confirmation response is lost. A client retry can then create a duplicate if the server treats each request as an unrelated command. Async error handling must distinguish failure to observe a result from proof that no effect occurred.',sections:[['Separate operation outcome from transport observation','A timeout indicates the caller stopped waiting; it does not universally cancel the underlying database or remote operation. Cancellation support varies by adapter and must be explicit. If the outcome is unknown, reconciliation or a status resource may be more honest than declaring failure with no effect.','For retryable state-changing commands, an idempotency key can identify the same logical operation. Store the key, validated request fingerprint and result atomically with the effect under the intended database contract. A process-local Map is only a teaching model and fails across restarts or replicas.'],['Bound retries and preserve useful failures','Retry only outcomes permitted by the operation contract, with a deadline, attempt budget and backoff policy. Do not automatically retry validation or authorization failures. Avoid sending a second response if the first response has already committed; preserve diagnostic context for uncertain outcomes.','The fixture demonstrates replay of a single synthetic key and refusal to apply the same effect twice in one process. It has no durable transaction and does not prove concurrent uniqueness. Real implementations need a database constraint, atomic state transition and integration tests for simultaneous duplicates.']],problem:'Model a replayed command and show its effect count remains one.',steps:['Look up a logical command key.','Record a synthetic result on first use.','Return the same result on a replay.'],code:'const results=new Map();let effects=0;function apply(key){if(results.has(key))return results.get(key);const result={id:++effects};results.set(key,result);return result;}\nconsole.log(apply("k1").id,apply("k1").id,effects);',expect:'1 1 1',result:'A sequential in-memory replay avoids a second modeled effect. Durability, request fingerprints and concurrent atomicity remain outside this fixture.',mistakes:['A response timeout does not prove a write rolled back.','A local replay map is not durable multi-replica idempotency.'],takeaways:['Represent uncertain outcomes honestly.','Pair retry policy with durable idempotency at the effect boundary.'],practice:[
'A response is lost after a committed enrollment write. What is the operation outcome to the caller?|Uncertain until reconciled|Certainly rolled back|Certainly never started|Automatically duplicated|Loss of observation does not establish whether the effect occurred.',
'What should an idempotency key identify?|One logical command|Every user forever|The database password|A random retry attempt only|Retries of the same logical action should share its stable identity.',
'Why is a Map insufficient for replicated durable replay protection?|It is process-local volatile state|It is always encrypted|It automatically coordinates replicas|It persists every transaction|Another process or restart does not share the same Map.',
'Which failures should not be blindly retried?|Validation and authorization failures|Every temporary connection failure always|Only successful reads|Only completed checks|Retries cannot repair a request that violates its input or access contract.'],formal:[
'What does a client timeout establish?|The client stopped waiting|The database necessarily rolled back|The server never received the request|The command had no effect|A timeout concerns observation and does not by itself prove the effect outcome.',
'An idempotency store has the same key with a different request fingerprint. What should the contract generally do?|Reject conflicting reuse|Apply both without distinction|Return an unrelated success|Ignore all body fields|A stable key must not silently identify conflicting operations.',
'Two replicas process the same retry key concurrently. Which boundary must enforce uniqueness?|A shared atomic durable store|Each replica’s private Map|A browser variable|A source comment|Cross-replica correctness requires coordination at the persisted effect boundary.',
'A repository promise times out while a write may continue. What should error handling avoid?|Unproved no-effect claims|Recording a safe request id|Exposing an uncertain outcome|Using reconciliation|A wait deadline does not universally cancel the underlying operation.',
'An enrollment client retries every 400 response ten times. What policy defect exists?|Retrying invalid input|The retries guarantee recovery|HTTP 400 always means timeout|The server must accept invalid input|A validation failure requires correcting the command rather than repeating it.',
'An idempotency key is recorded only after a separate write commits. What failure window remains?|Effect committed without replay record|Guaranteed atomicity|Automatic distributed rollback|A completed response before every effect|Effect and replay record must share an atomic persistence contract to close the gap.',
'An enrollment commits, then its response is lost and another replica receives the same key with changed body data. Which persistence contract avoids both duplicate effects and conflicting replay?|Atomic key and fingerprint|A private Map per replica|A new key for every retry|Only a client-side cache|The shared durable boundary must atomically connect key, request fingerprint and effect/result, rejecting changed-command reuse and coordinating concurrent replicas.']},
{comp:'m4.c1',title:'Own a bounded database connection pool',before:'Know async cleanup and database connections. A pool leases finite connections to concurrent operations.',intro:'An orders service should share a bounded pool for its process rather than open a fresh unbounded connection for every route. The pool manages leases, not business transactions automatically. A leaked lease can eventually block unrelated requests even when the database is healthy.',sections:[['Set a capacity budget across the application','Pool limits apply per process or adapter instance. Ten replicas with a maximum of twenty connections can demand two hundred server connections, before administration or background jobs. Choose limits from the database budget, expected concurrency and queue latency, then measure acquisition time.','An operation may use a convenience query API that leases automatically, while a transaction needs one dedicated connection for its complete sequence. Do not run BEGIN through one random pool query and COMMIT through another; those calls might use different sessions. Consult the concrete driver contract.'],['Release ownership even after exceptions','Acquire a connection inside a controlled lifetime and release it in finally. A successful query and a failed query both end the lease. Roll back an unsuccessful explicit transaction before returning a reusable connection, according to the driver behavior. Never store request-specific session state on a pooled connection without resetting it safely.','The model below records one lease and release on a synthetic failure. It does not connect to SQL or MongoDB. Real pooling evidence must use the chosen driver and isolated database, including exhaustion, recovery and transaction-affinity tests.']],problem:'Show that failure still returns a synthetic database lease.',steps:['Acquire a fixture connection.','Trigger a controlled operation failure.','Release in finally and inspect counters.'],code:'(async()=>{let leased=0,released=0;const pool={acquire:async()=>{leased++;return {release:()=>released++};}};const c=await pool.acquire();try{throw Error("query failed");}catch(e){console.log(e.message);}finally{c.release();}console.log(leased,released);})();',expect:'query failed\n1 1',result:'The synthetic lease is released after failure. No real pool sizing, SQL session or MongoDB driver behavior is claimed.',mistakes:['A pool limit multiplied by replicas can exceed database capacity.','Transaction statements must share their session.'],takeaways:['Budget connection capacity across every replica.','Release each lease through failure-safe cleanup.'],practice:[
'What is a connection pool lease?|A temporary connection|An automatic schema migration|A guaranteed transaction commit|A public API key|A pool lends a finite resource for a bounded operation.',
'Where should a manually acquired connection normally be released?|In finally|Only after successful output|Only when the process exits|In a public response body|Cleanup must execute on success and failure.',
'Why must transaction statements use one dedicated connection?|Transaction state belongs to its session|All SQL strings are identical|Pools encrypt statements|Every query is automatically a transaction|Random pool queries can select different sessions.',
'Ten replicas each allow twenty pool connections. What total maximum must be considered?|Two hundred before other clients|Twenty across the whole cluster|Ten automatically|One shared connection|Per-process budgets multiply across replicas.'],formal:[
'What resource does a database pool bound?|Database connections|Every frontend component|Git object storage|All HTTP methods|Pool configuration limits available database sessions for its adapter instance.',
'Why is connection acquisition latency useful operational evidence?|It can reveal pool contention|It proves all SQL is correct|It authenticates the caller|It removes the need for cleanup|Waiting to obtain a lease can expose exhausted or undersized capacity.',
'A route releases its connection only when query succeeds. What long-term symptom can occur?|Pool exhaustion after failures|Infinite available connections|Guaranteed rollback of every write|Automatic leak repair by Express|Leaked leases reduce usable pool capacity for later requests.',
'A transaction uses pool.query for BEGIN then separate pool.query calls for updates and COMMIT. What is unsafe?|Session affinity is not established|SQL cannot have transactions|The JSON body is too small|All pooled queries are read-only|A transaction must remain on the same leased connection.',
'A team scales from two replicas to twenty without changing pool limits. What must be revisited?|Total connection budget|Only route capitalization|Only the package description|The number of CSS classes|Horizontal API scale can multiply database connection demand.',
'A pooled session retains a request-specific setting for the next user. What boundary needs correction?|Session-state isolation|Only the API hostname|Promise result ordering|The Git author name|Reused connections must not leak request-specific state between leases.',
'A fake pool cleanup test passes. What remains outside its evidence?|Real session behavior|The fixture release count|The executed finally block|The caught synthetic message|A local model cannot establish the real adapter lifecycle.']},
{comp:'m4.c2',title:'Bind SQL values and allowlist query structure',before:'Use SQL predicates and distinguish a query template from a value. Parameter markers differ by driver.',intro:'A support search API should never concatenate an email address into SQL. Parameter binding keeps input values separate from query syntax. It does not make every dynamic query fragment safe, so column names and sort directions need a separate allowlist.',sections:[['Keep code and data separate','For a PostgreSQL-style adapter, a query can use WHERE email = $1 and pass an array of values. The driver sends the value through its parameter protocol. Do not insert quotes around the placeholder as though it were a string literal; follow the actual driver interface.','Parameters generally stand for values, not arbitrary identifiers or keywords. If a client chooses a sort field, map it through a fixed dictionary to a known SQL identifier and select direction through an allowed set. Do not concatenate unchecked user input into ORDER BY.'],['Preserve authorization and type rules','Parameterization prevents input values from becoming SQL structure; it does not establish object ownership, input size policy or correct transaction boundaries. Include the trusted tenant or user scope in the query when the access contract requires it. A valid id without the correct scope can still expose another tenant’s data.','The example constructs a query plan and prints that the untrusted email remains a parameter. No SQL engine executes it; the educational claim is separation of values and structure. Production evidence must execute the chosen driver against isolated data and verify permissions as well as binding.']],problem:'Build a fixed SQL template with an allowlisted sort identifier.',steps:['Map the requested sort token to an approved column.','Keep the email entirely in the parameter array.','Inspect the template and parameter count.'],code:'function plan(email,sort){const columns={created:"created_at",name:"display_name"};if(!Object.hasOwn(columns,sort))throw Error("invalid sort");return {text:"SELECT id FROM users WHERE email = $1 ORDER BY "+columns[sort],values:[email]};}\nconst p=plan("x\' OR TRUE --","created");console.log(p.text);console.log(p.values.length,p.values[0].includes("OR TRUE"));',expect:'SELECT id FROM users WHERE email = $1 ORDER BY created_at\n1 true',result:'Suspicious text remains a value. The fixture does not prove driver execution or database access control.',mistakes:['Placeholders cannot safely stand in for arbitrary SQL identifiers.','A bound id without tenant scope can still violate authorization.'],takeaways:['Bind values through the actual driver API.','Allowlist structural choices and preserve trusted scope.'],practice:[
'Which part of a SQL request should contain a supplied email value?|The parameter array|An interpolated SQL fragment|A dynamic column name|The connection password|Binding separates untrusted data from SQL syntax.',
'Can a value placeholder generally choose an arbitrary ORDER BY column?|No; allowlist columns|Yes, all SQL grammar is a value|Yes, if the input is long|Only after logging the password|Identifiers are query structure and need a controlled mapping.',
'Why include trusted tenant scope in a row lookup?|Enforce object isolation|Replace every input validator|Increase SQL string length|Guarantee database uptime|Injection prevention alone does not limit which tenant rows may be returned.',
'What is wrong with WHERE email = \'$1\' in a PostgreSQL parameterized template?|It is a quoted literal|It validates every email|It adds tenant isolation|It creates a connection pool|Parameter syntax must follow the driver protocol rather than quoting a placeholder as ordinary text.',
'A client asks for sort field password_hash. What should the sort allowlist do?|Reject the unapproved field|Concatenate it directly|Return the hash automatically|Skip authorization|Only explicitly permitted structural choices should reach SQL.',
'An attacker string is in a values array beside fixed SQL. What does the local model prove?|Separation in the constructed plan|Actual database execution safety universally|Correct schema indexes|Valid user ownership|A plan fixture demonstrates construction, while the real driver still needs integration evidence.',
'Which statement about parameter binding is accurate?|It does not replace authorization|It authorizes every numeric id|It disables database constraints|It makes every query fast|Value binding and access policy address different failure modes.',
'A query adds unchecked ASC or DESC text from the request. What must change?|Validate against the allowed directions|Quote the whole SQL program|Trust it because values are bound elsewhere|Remove every WHERE clause|Dynamic keywords require a controlled structural choice.'],formal:[
'What does parameter binding protect at the query boundary?|Separation of values from SQL syntax|All tenant permissions automatically|The correctness of every index|The availability of the database|Binding prevents supplied values from being interpreted as query structure.',
'Which query element normally requires a structural allowlist rather than a value binding?|A selected sort column|A searched email|A minimum numeric amount|A tenant id value|Column identifiers are syntax and must be chosen from trusted structure.',
'A tenant-aware route safely binds id but omits tenant_id from the lookup. What vulnerability remains?|Cross-tenant object access|A guaranteed syntax error|Automatic transaction rollback|No possible data access|Safe SQL values do not imply correct authorization scope.',
'A developer escapes apostrophes manually instead of using the driver API. What is the stronger boundary?|Driver parameterization|A longer string replace chain|Logging the raw query publicly|Removing all input limits|Maintained parameter protocols separate syntax and data more reliably than ad hoc concatenation.',
'A test inspects a query plan but never invokes the SQL driver. What is still needed?|An isolated binding integration test|Only a screenshot of the SQL|Another identical string comparison|A larger sample prompt|Plan construction alone cannot verify the actual driver protocol.',
'A reporting endpoint permits only created_at and display_name sorting. What should an unknown token produce?|A controlled validation outcome|Unchecked SQL concatenation|An automatic admin query|A public schema dump|Unknown structural choices must not become arbitrary query fragments.',
'A query binds all values correctly but returns too many columns including password_hash. What correction is needed?|A safe response and query projection|A different parameter marker only|A larger connection pool|More retry attempts|Parameterization does not prevent disclosure through an overbroad selected projection.']},
{comp:'m4.c1',title:'Choose data access boundaries for SQL and MongoDB',before:'Know leases and parameterized queries. Document databases and SQL drivers have distinct APIs and consistency contracts.',intro:'A Node API can use a relational pool or a MongoDB client, but the HTTP handler should depend on a deliberate repository interface rather than leak driver details everywhere. Reusing a managed client avoids uncontrolled connection creation; it does not erase the database’s transaction, schema or consistency semantics.',sections:[['Keep the repository contract narrow','A repository can expose findVisibleOrder(principal, id) or reserveStock(command) rather than accept arbitrary query objects from the route. This keeps trusted scope and atomic operation requirements explicit. A convenience abstraction must not split one required atomic transition into unrelated driver calls.','MongoDB drivers manage their own connection pools; client lifetime should follow documented process-level reuse and shutdown rules. ODM validation can enforce selected application rules, but raw driver writes or updates may follow different validation paths. Do not assume an ODM automatically enforces every business invariant on every write.'],['Test the real semantics at the adapter boundary','SQL uniqueness and MongoDB document/update behavior need isolated engine tests using synthetic data. A fake repository is useful for HTTP projection and error paths, but cannot establish query syntax, write concern or transaction behavior. Record the actual database and driver versions when collecting that evidence.','A safe route can select public fields even if the repository contains internal metadata. Projection reduces disclosure and stabilizes the API contract. The local model exercises that adapter shape only; selecting MongoDB here does not automatically prove the wider MERN pathway or deployed integration.']],problem:'Project public order data from an injected repository.',steps:['Use a repository with a declared lookup method.','Call it with trusted principal scope.','Return only the API-owned fields.'],code:'(async()=>{const repo={findVisible:async(user,id)=>user==="u1"?{id,total:42,internal:"private"}:null};async function read(user,id){const row=await repo.findVisible(user,id);return row?{status:200,body:{id:row.id,total:row.total}}:{status:404};}console.log(JSON.stringify(await read("u1","o1")));console.log((await read("u2","o1")).status);})();',expect:'{"status":200,"body":{"id":"o1","total":42}}\n404',result:'The fake adapter demonstrates scope and public projection. Real SQL or MongoDB semantics are not executed.',mistakes:['A repository abstraction does not create atomicity automatically.','ODM validation coverage must be checked for the actual write path.'],takeaways:['Reuse managed clients and expose narrow repository operations.','Verify engine semantics separately from route-shaped models.'],practice:[
'Why prefer a process-managed database client over one new client per request?|Bound and reuse connection resources|Guarantee every write is authorized|Eliminate all validation|Make all reads synchronous|Managed client lifetime prevents uncontrolled connection creation.',
'What should a narrow repository interface make explicit?|Required scope and operation semantics|Every frontend style|The production password|All arbitrary client query operators|A deliberate interface exposes access and atomicity expectations.',
'Can a fake repository establish MongoDB write concern behavior?|No, the real adapter boundary is absent|Yes, any JavaScript object is MongoDB|Yes, if it returns JSON|Only when the id is short|A substitute does not execute the target engine or driver.',
'Why project public fields before sending a response?|Avoid exposing internal metadata|Create a database transaction|Release every pool lease|Authorize every possible user|A stable allowlisted response controls disclosure at the API boundary.'],formal:[
'What must be checked before relying on ODM validation?|Write-path validation|Only the model class name|Only the HTTP route count|Only the package description|Different write paths can have different validation coverage.',
'A route forwards a client-supplied MongoDB query object directly to a repository. What design improves the boundary?|Construct an allowlisted scoped query|Trust every operator key|Allow unrestricted filters for convenience|Skip resource authorization|The route must control query structure and trusted access scope.',
'A repository exposes reserveStock but performs separate unprotected read and write calls. What property is unproved?|Atomic reservation|The method exists|The route can invoke it|The object has fields|A method name does not enforce the required concurrency semantics.',
'A pool fixture passes but the target engine rejects an update syntax. What evidence was missing?|Real adapter integration|Another fake response projection|A larger route file|A public demo screenshot|Driver and engine semantics require execution at their actual boundary.',
'A response accidentally includes an internal audit field. Which layer can enforce the public shape?|An explicit API projection|A larger pool limit|A shorter database name|An unbounded retry loop|Selecting public response fields prevents unintended internal disclosure.',
'A service closes its shared database client after every request. What needs reconsideration?|Client lifecycle ownership|Only the response phrase|The numeric id width|The Git branch name|A reused managed client should be closed by its owning lifecycle, not by unrelated request cleanup.',
'A team switches repositories from SQL to MongoDB behind one interface. What must still be tested?|New adapter semantics|Only JavaScript object names|Only existing fake tests|Nothing if method names match|A shared interface does not guarantee identical persistence or concurrency behavior.']}
];
units.forEach((u,i)=>{u.difficulties=i<6?['E','E','M','M','M','H','H','A']:i<9?['E','M','M','H','H','H','A']:i<11?['E','M','M','M','H','H','H']:['E','M','M','H','H','H','H'];});
const refs=[ref('node-loop','nodejs/node',NODE,'doc/api/timers.md','Node 22 timers and event-loop thresholds'),ref('node-modules','nodejs/node',NODE,'doc/api/modules.md','Node 22 CommonJS modules and caching'),ref('node-streams','nodejs/node',NODE,'doc/api/stream.md','Node 22 stream backpressure'),ref('express-app','expressjs/express',EXPRESS,'lib/application.js','Express 4.21.2 application implementation'),ref('express-router','expressjs/express',EXPRESS,'lib/router/index.js','Express 4.21.2 router dispatch'),ref('express-layer','expressjs/express',EXPRESS,'lib/router/layer.js','Express 4.21.2 handler and error signatures')];
const built=build('node-express',units,refs);module.exports=built.course;Object.defineProperty(module.exports,'formal',{value:built.formal});
